# NEWGRP(1)

## NAME

newgrp - log in to a new group

## SYNOPSIS

| **newgrp** group

## DESCRIPTION

*Newgrp* changes the group identification of its caller, analogously to login(1). The same
person remains logged in, and the current directory is unchanged, but calculations of access
permissions to files are performed with respect to the new group ID.

A password is demanded if the group has a password and the user himself does not.

When most users log in, they are members of the group named `other.`

**Note:** the caller must be listed among the group's members in group(5), with one exception:
the group literally named `other` is exempt from that test, which is what the paragraph above
is really about. On this system `other` is group 1 and the one ordinary account, **guest**,
logs in as group 3.

**Note:** *newgrp* is *not* known to this shell, and sh(1) forks for it like any other command.
The consequence is that the new group lasts only until that shell is left, and that the login
shell underneath it is unaffected.

**Note:** every outcome, including every refusal, ends by executing a shell. *Newgrp* has
replaced nothing at that point - the shell that forked it is still waiting - but a refusal that
simply exited would be indistinguishable from one that worked, and on a login shell it would
end the session. So `Sorry` or `no such group` is followed by a prompt, with the group
unchanged. The exit status cannot report any of this and does not try.

**Note:** *newgrp* is set-user-id to root, and narrowly: setgid(2) admits a group the caller is
already in, or the super-user, and the whole point of the command is the case that is neither.
The privilege is given back with setuid(2) before the shell is executed.

## FILES

/etc/group, /etc/passwd

## SEE ALSO

login(1), su(1), passwd(1), group(5), setuid(2)
